course: dictionary
lesson: 11851
title: Boarder: one word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A boarder is someone who lives away from home for a while. The word has several meanings, so look at the context, the words around it, to find the right one. «Maya is a boarder at Hillcrest School, so she sleeps there during term.» The clues are school and term, so here a boarder is a pupil who lives at school.
explain: «Mrs Brown takes in boarders to earn some money.» The clues are takes in and earn money, so a boarder here is a person who pays to live and eat in someone else's house. The opposite of a boarder at school is a day pupil, who goes home every evening.
explain: «The boarders swung onto the enemy ship, swords raised.» In pirate stories, a boarder is a person who climbs onto a ship to attack it. To check your meaning, put it into the sentence and ask: does it still make sense?
fact: «Boarder» means a pupil who lives at a boarding school during term instead of going home each day.
fact: «Boarder» can also mean a person who pays to live and eat meals in someone else's house.
fact: Informally, a boarder is a person who rides a skateboard, snowboard or surfboard.
fact: In old pirate stories, a boarder is a person who climbs onto an enemy ship to attack or take it over.
fact: The opposite of a boarder at school is a day pupil, who goes home every day.
exercise: «Tom sleeps at Hillcrest School from Monday to Friday and goes home at weekends.» What is Tom? | A day pupil who goes home each evening | A pirate on a ship | *A boarder at a boarding school | A skateboarder
exercise: «The old pirates were boarders, leaping across to the enemy ship.» Which meaning fits here? | A person who lives in someone's house for pay | A person who rides a surfboard | *A person who climbs onto a ship to attack it | A pupil who stays at school
exercise: «Grandma takes in boarders, so two students live in her spare room and pay rent.» What does boarder mean here? | *A person who pays to live and eat in someone's house | A pupil at a boarding school | A skateboarder | A pirate
test: «Sam rides his snowboard down the hill every winter.» What kind of boarder is Sam? | A pirate on a ship | *A person who rides a snowboard | A pupil who lives at school | A person who pays for a room
test: Which word means the opposite of a boarder at school? | A lodger | A ship's captain | *A day pupil | A pirate
exam: Which sentence uses boarder to mean a pupil who lives at school? | *«The boarders all woke at seven for the bell in the dormitory.» | «The boarder paid rent to the old lady downstairs.» | «The boarder climbed the enemy ship with a cutlass.» | «The boarder rode her snowboard down the mountain.»
